\chapter{Abstracts from Europe PMC}
\label{europepmc-module}

The search providers do not hold an abstract for every record.
In one screen, three of the abstracts the screeners had to look up by
hand were on PubMed, for journal articles whose records lacked them
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/260}).
This chapter asks Europe PMC for them.
Europe PMC's web service answers a search by DOI with the work's
record, abstract included, and needs no key; the life-science
literature that PubMed indexes is what it holds, so one service covers
that ground.
We do not also ask PubMed's own E-utilities: a second service for the
same literature would add requests and a second set of terms for little
gain.

Like Unpaywall (\cref{unpaywall-module}), Europe PMC is not a search
provider here but a per-DOI lookup: enrichment (\cref{enrich-module})
asks it, after the search providers and Crossref
(\cref{sec:crossref-abstract}), for a paper that still has no abstract,
and it fills the abstract and nothing else.

\section{What the service documents}

The facts below come from the service's own OpenAPI description,
served by the API at
\url{https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/v3/api-docs}
(read 2026-11-20), and from two recorded answers.
Europe PMC's human-readable pages (\url{https://europepmc.org/RestfulWebService},
the reference guide PDF and the copyright page under
\url{https://europepmc.org/}) answered with a bot challenge on that day
and were not read; what only they would say is left out.
\begin{description}
\item[The search] [[GET
  https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search]] with a
  [[query]].  A fielded search is written [[field:value]], a phrase in
  speech marks, and the [[fields]] module of the same API lists
  [[DOI]] among the search fields (read 2026-11-20).  So the query for
  one DOI is [[DOI:"10.7717/peerj.4375"]].
\item[The abstract] [[resultType]] [[core]] \enquote{returns full
  metadata for a given publication ID; including abstract, full text
  links, and MeSH terms}, the default [[lite]] only \enquote{key
  metadata}; the record's field is [[abstractText]], a string.
\item[Format and size] [[format=json]]; [[pageSize]] defaults to 25.
\item[An address] The optional [[email]] parameter \enquote{registers
  the user's email address with EBI. The user can then be contacted
  about Web Service related news.}  Registering the user for news is
  not ours to do, so we never send it.
\end{description}

The recorded answers (2026-11-20, the query above and the same for
[[10.1109/CVPR.2016.90]], [[resultType=core]], [[format=json]],
[[pageSize=5]], no address sent) show the two outcomes: one result
under the DOI with a plain-text [[abstractText]], and [[hitCount]] 0
with an empty [[resultList.result]] for a DOI the service does not
hold.
That a DOI search returns only the work with that DOI is not
documented; we check each result's [[doi]] against ours and take the
first that matches and has an abstract.

\paragraph{Rate.}
The OpenAPI description states no rate limit.
The only figure we found is a reply in Europe PMC's web-services forum,
\enquote{we can't increase the current rate limit of 10 requests per
second (or 500 per minute)}, adding that \enquote{exceeding the limit
queues your requests, and excessive traffic may result in timeouts}
(\url{https://groups.google.com/a/ebi.ac.uk/g/epmc-webservices/c/x3PLL09mels},
posted 2024-10-01, read 2026-11-20).
A forum reply is not documentation, so the pace is scholar's own
choice: five requests per second, half that figure, the same pace for
any user, and slow enough that a burst of lookups stays far from it.
A 429 is still handled, through the shared limiter, though the service
documents none.

\paragraph{Terms.}
The terms of use of EMBL-EBI, which runs the service, say that
\enquote{where EMBL-EBI presents scientific data generated by others,
EMBL-EBI imposes no additional restriction on the use of the
contributed data than those provided by the data owner} and that
\enquote{the original data may be subject to rights claimed by third
parties, including [\dots] copyright}; they also warn that
\enquote{any attempt to use EMBL-EBI Data Resources and Tools to a
level that prevents, or is likely to prevent, EMBL-EBI providing
services to others, will result in the user being blocked}
(\url{https://www.ebi.ac.uk/about/terms-of-use}, last revised
2026-10-14, read 2026-11-20).
An abstract is the publisher's or the authors' text; scholar keeps it
in the user's own session for screening, with Europe PMC named in
[[Paper.sources]], as it keeps those the search providers return.
Europe PMC's own copyright page was among those not read.

